"Proofs and disproofs of some conjectures on zero-sum problems of new types (https://arxiv.t3mt.com/abs/2610.19965)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Title:Proofs and disproofs of some conjectures on zero-sum problems of new types ... View a PDF of the paper titled Proofs and disproofs of some conjectures on zero-sum problems of new types, by Yang Li and 3 other authors\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 14 Oct 2026]\n\n# Title:Proofs and disproofs of some conjectures on zero-sum problems of new types\n\nAuthors:Yang Li, San Ling, Zhonghua Sun, Shixin Zhu\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Proofs and disproofs of some conjectures on zero-sum problems of new types, by Yang Li and 3 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:For integers $r\\ge1$ and $n\\ge2$, let $s_r(n)$ be the least $N$ such that every sequence of $N$ vectors in $\\Z^r$, each nonzero modulo $n$, contains $n$ terms whose sum is zero modulo $n$ but nonzero modulo $n^2$. Let $w_r(n)$ be the corresponding least $N$ when any nonempty subsequence is allowed. These constants were introduced as zero-sum problems of new types by Z.-W. Sun. We prove $s_r(n)\\ge(2^{r+1}-2)(n-1)+1$ by constructing an explicit sequence from a lattice polytope, \\emph{disproving} his conjecture $s_2(n)=4n+1$ for $n\\ge4$. We obtain $w_r(2)=2r+1$ by classifying all sequences avoiding such nonempty subsequences modulo $4$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProofs and disproofs of some conjectures on zero-sum problems of new types (https://www.paperpeel.com/paper/2610.19965)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This paper aims to study the zero-sum problems of new types and to answer Sun’s conjectures and questions listed above. ... Note also that analogous classification questions for classical zero-sum constants were studied as inverse zero-sum problems in [6, 19]. ...   * r=2 and n=2 k 3 ℓ p by Theorem 1.2(iii), where p is a prime and k,ℓ≥0 are integers,\n## 1 Introduction\n\ni∈I∑​a i​≡0(mod n).\n\nNote that the condition in (1.1) depends only on residues modulo n and does not determine whether the selected sum also vanishes modulo n 2. For prime moduli, Gao et al. [7] studied exact divisibility conditions for zero-sum subsequences. For a prime p, the p-adic valuation of an integer x is v p​(x)=sup{k∈Z:\\penalty k≥0 and p k∣x}. For r=1 and n=p, their constant s(p,1) coincides with the least length such that every integer sequence of that length whose terms are not divisible by p contains a p-term subsequence whose sum has p-adic valuation exactly 1. Equivalently, the selected sum must vanish modulo p but not modulo p 2. Recently, it was shown in [8] that s(p,1)≤3 p−2 for every prime p, improving the bound s(p,1)≤s(Z p 2​)=4 p−3 obtained from Kemnitz’s theorem [11, 18].\n\ni∈I∑​a i​≡0(mod n),but i∈I∑​a i​≡0(mod n 2).\n\nBy definition, we immediately have w r​(n)≤s r​(n) and s 1​(p)=s(p,1). For n>2, Sun proved that 2 r n+1≤s r​(n)≤n(n r−1)+1 in [20, Theorem 1.3]. For r=2, Sun conjectured in the same paper that w 2​(n)=4 n−3 for every n>1 in Conjecture 1.1(ii) and s 2​(n)=4 n+1 for every n>2 in Conjecture 1.2. In Remark 1.2, he also asked whether w r​(n)=s(Z n r​) for all integers n>1 and r>0. More related constants and conjectures can also be found in [7, 20].\n\nThis paper aims to study the zero-sum problems of new types and to answer Sun’s conjectures and questions listed above. In the following, we call an admissible sequence bad if no n-term subsequence satisfies (1.2), and w-bad if no nonempty subsequence does. Such sequences give lower bounds for s r​(n) and w r​(n), respectively. We write ∣A∣ for the length of A, σ(A) for the sum of its terms in the ambient abelian group, and v[m] for m copies of v. In addition, sequence products mean concatenation and an empty product denotes the empty sequence.\n\nOur first main result addresses Sun’s fixed-length conjecture through a construction from lattice polytopes.\n\nA r,n​=v∈V r​∏​v[n−1],\\penalty with\\penalty V r​={±1 J​:\\penalty ∅=J⊆[r]}\\penalty and\\penalty 1 J​=j∈J∑​e j​\n\n  * In [20, Theorem 1.3(ii)], Sun proved s r​(n)≥2 r n+1 for r≥1 and n>2. Our bound is strictly stronger for r≥2 and n>2, except (r,n)=(2,3), where the bounds coincide. For r=2 and n≥4, the bound (1.4) gives\n  * For r≥2, Sun also proved 3⋅2 r+1≤s r​(3)≤2⋅3 r−1 in [20, Theorem 1.4(i)]. Our bound s r​(3)≥4⋅2 r−3 improves the lower bound by 2 r−4, with strict improvement for r≥3.\n\nOur second main result presents some exact values for w r​(n) and solves Sun’s rank-two conjecture for certain moduli, which derives from a complete classification of all w-bad sequences of arbitrary lengths for n=2 and r≥1, and a complete classification of all w-bad sequences of length 4 p−4 modulo p 2 for n=p and r=2. During the process, we also obtain doubling and tripling inequalities to propagate the result w 2​(t) to w 2​(d t) for d∈{2,3}.\n\n  1. For every integer r≥1, we have w r​(2)=2 r+1.\n  2. For d∈{2,3} and every integer t>1, we have w 2​(d t)≤d w 2​(t)+3(d−1).\n  3. For every prime p and integers k,ℓ≥0, we have w 2​(2 k 3 ℓ p)=4⋅2 k 3 ℓ p−3.\n\n  * Since s(Z 2 r​)=2 r+1 [9], Theorem 1.2(i) answers this question negatively for every r≥3.\n  * Since s(Z n 2​)=4 n−3 for every n≥2 by [11, 18], Theorem 1.2(iii) answers this question affirmatively in rank two for these moduli.\n\nOur third main result shows that this failure of the equality in Sun’s question, as stated in Remark 1.2, is not confined to the binary case.\n\nw r​(n)≤n⌈r lo g 2​n⌉(⌈r lo g 2​n⌉+1)+1,\n\nTheorem 1.3 answers Sun’s equality question [20, Remark 1.2] negatively for every fixed modulus n and all sufficiently large ranks r. In particular, the limit strengthens this conclusion to an asymptotic separation between w r​(n) and the classical Erdős-Ginzburg-Ziv constant s(Z n r​).\n\nThe rest of the pa