und on w r​(n) by constructing a w-bad sequence in Z r of length 2 r(n−1) as follows.\n\nE r,n​=j=1∏r​e j[n−1]​(−e j​)[n−1]\n\nBy construction, E r,n​ is admissible. For a subsequence, let u j​ and v j​ count the selected copies of e j​ and −e j​, respectively. Since 0≤u j​,v j​≤n−1, u j​−v j​ is divisible by n only when u j​=v j​. Therefore, every subsequence of E r,n​ with sum zero modulo n has sum zero in Z r, and E r,n​ is w-bad. Since ∣E r,n​∣=2 r(n−1), we obtain w r​(n)≥2 r(n−1)+1. ∎\n\nWe now prove Theorem 1.2(i) by showing that 2 r+1 is also an upper bound for w r​(2). For this purpose, the following theorem classifies all nonempty w-bad sequences in Z r for n=2, with the normal form specified modulo 4.\n\nA=x 1​⋯x d​y 1​⋯y k​,\\penalty with\\penalty y j​≡−i∈S j​∑​x i​(mod 4)\\penalty for\\penalty 1≤j≤k,\n\n0≡y j​+y ℓ​+i∈S j​△S ℓ​∑​x i​≡−i∈S j​∑​x i​−i∈S ℓ​∑​x i​+i∈S j​△S ℓ​∑​x i​=−2 i∈S j​∩S ℓ​∑​x i​(mod 4),\n\nσ(B)=j∈J∑​(y j​+i∈S j​∑​x i​)≡0(mod 4).\n\nUsing the disjointness and nonemptiness of the sets S j​ in Theorem 3.1, we obtain k≤d≤r and ∣A∣=d+k≤2 d≤2 r for every w-bad sequence A. It follows that w r​(2)≤2 r+1. On the other hand, taking n=2 in Lemma 3.1 yields w r​(2)≥2 r+1. We therefore conclude that w r​(2)=2 r+1 for every r≥1. ∎\n\n### 3.3 Proofs of Theorems 1.2(ii) and 1.2(iii)\n\nWe now prove Theorem 1.2(ii) and then combine it with Corollary 3.1 to obtain part (iii). For the part of the tripling inequality in Theorem 1.2(ii), we also need the following length bound and the structure of sequences attaining it.\n\nA≡U[2](−U)[2]V[2](−V)[2](U+V)[2](−U−V)[2](mod 9).\n\nS={a mod 3:\\penalty a is a term of A}⊆F 3 2​∖{0}.\n\nSuppose that four terms x 1​,…,x 4​ of A have the same residue u∈S modulo 3. Then ∑j=i​x j​≡0(mod 9) for 1≤i≤4. By subtraction, we have x 1​≡x 2​≡x 3​≡x 4​(mod 9) and hence, 3 x 1​≡0(mod 9), contrary to admissibility. Thus, each element of S is represented by at most three terms of A.\n\nS∩(−S)=∅,−a−b∈/S,\\penalty ∀\\penalty a,b∈S